1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,386
To certify a new jet engine,

2
00:00:02,386 --> 00:00:05,284
engineers run it at full power and

3
00:00:05,284 --> 00:00:07,927
then blow one of its own blades

4
00:00:07,927 --> 00:00:10,228
off with a small explosive.

5
00:00:10,408 --> 00:00:12,441
They fire birds into it with a

6
00:00:12,441 --> 00:00:12,916
cannon.

7
00:00:13,096 --> 00:00:15,156
They let gas flow through it that

8
00:00:15,156 --> 00:00:17,340
is hotter than the melting point of

9
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:18,401
the metal inside.

10
00:00:18,581 --> 00:00:20,479
And it has to survive all of

11
00:00:20,479 --> 00:00:20,683
it.

12
00:00:20,863 --> 00:00:23,978
Today, eleven parts of a jet engine

13
00:00:23,978 --> 00:00:26,737
that almost nobody understands,

14
00:00:26,737 --> 00:00:28,874
and why each one exists.

15
00:00:29,054 --> 00:00:31,605
Number ten is the reason an engine

16
00:00:31,605 --> 00:00:34,081
is designed to fall off the wing.

17
00:00:34,431 --> 00:00:37,069
A modern airliner engine is one of

18
00:00:37,069 --> 00:00:40,096
the most extreme machines people build.

19
00:00:40,276 --> 00:00:42,703
It swallows a huge amount of air

20
00:00:42,703 --> 00:00:44,675
every second, squeezes it,

21
00:00:44,675 --> 00:00:45,888
sets it on fire,

22
00:00:45,888 --> 00:00:47,936
and throws it out the back,

23
00:00:47,936 --> 00:00:50,969
for thousands of hours without stopping.

24
00:00:51,149 --> 00:00:53,403
Every visible feature on it,

25
00:00:53,403 --> 00:00:55,093
and many hidden ones,

26
00:00:55,093 --> 00:00:57,991
is the answer to a specific problem,

27
00:00:57,991 --> 00:00:59,762
whether that is noise,

28
00:00:59,762 --> 00:01:02,418
heat, birds, or the worst case of

29
00:01:02,418 --> 00:01:05,155
all, a part breaking loose at full

30
00:01:05,155 --> 00:01:05,638
speed.

31
00:01:05,989 --> 00:01:09,388
As always, every number here comes from

32
00:01:09,388 --> 00:01:10,695
a named source,

33
00:01:10,695 --> 00:01:12,264
the engine makers,

34
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:16,448
NASA, the Federal Aviation Administration rules,

35
00:01:16,448 --> 00:01:19,586
or official accident investigations,

36
00:01:19,586 --> 00:01:22,113
and all of them are listed in

37
00:01:22,113 --> 00:01:23,508
the description.

38
00:01:23,688 --> 00:01:25,358
Let's start at the front,

39
00:01:25,358 --> 00:01:27,629
with the part you can actually see

40
00:01:27,629 --> 00:01:29,098
from your window seat,

41
00:01:29,098 --> 00:01:30,033
the giant fan,

42
00:01:30,033 --> 00:01:32,638
and the surprising fact about where the

43
00:01:32,638 --> 00:01:34,776
engine's push really comes from.

44
00:01:35,126 --> 00:01:37,246
Why is the fan at the front

45
00:01:37,246 --> 00:01:39,444
of a jet engine so enormous?

46
00:01:39,624 --> 00:01:41,818
Because the fan makes most of the

47
00:01:41,818 --> 00:01:42,283
thrust.

48
00:01:42,463 --> 00:01:44,649
In a modern high bypass engine,

49
00:01:44,649 --> 00:01:46,623
most of the air pulled in by

50
00:01:46,623 --> 00:01:49,303
the fan never goes through the burning

51
00:01:49,303 --> 00:01:50,149
core at all.

52
00:01:50,329 --> 00:01:51,778
It flows around it,

53
00:01:51,778 --> 00:01:52,922
through a duct,

54
00:01:52,922 --> 00:01:54,218
and out the back.

55
00:01:54,398 --> 00:01:57,154
That bypass air provides up to eighty

56
00:01:57,154 --> 00:01:59,240
percent of the total thrust.

57
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:01,357
Moving a lot of air a little

58
00:02:01,357 --> 00:02:04,194
faster is more efficient and quieter than

59
00:02:04,194 --> 00:02:06,270
moving a little air very fast.

60
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:08,772
That is why fans keep getting bigger.

61
00:02:09,122 --> 00:02:10,960
Look at the GE nine X,

62
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:13,968
the engine of the Boeing seven seven

63
00:02:13,968 --> 00:02:14,636
seven X.

64
00:02:14,816 --> 00:02:16,909
According to GE Aerospace,

65
00:02:16,909 --> 00:02:20,128
its front fan is one hundred thirty-four

66
00:02:20,128 --> 00:02:23,187
inches across, and its bypass ratio is

67
00:02:23,187 --> 00:02:24,555
about ten to one,

68
00:02:24,555 --> 00:02:27,694
meaning roughly ten times more air goes

69
00:02:27,694 --> 00:02:30,269
around the core than through it.

70
00:02:30,449 --> 00:02:32,504
It holds a world record of one

71
00:02:32,504 --> 00:02:36,065
hundred thirty-four thousand three hundred pounds of

72
00:02:36,065 --> 00:02:36,544
thrust.

73
00:02:36,895 --> 00:02:39,375
So the jet engine on a modern

74
00:02:39,375 --> 00:02:42,711
airliner is really two machines in one.

75
00:02:42,891 --> 00:02:46,143
A small, extremely hot gas turbine in

76
00:02:46,143 --> 00:02:48,955
the middle, and a giant fan that

77
00:02:48,955 --> 00:02:49,834
it drives.

78
00:02:50,014 --> 00:02:52,034
The hot core is the heart.

79
00:02:52,214 --> 00:02:54,060
But the fan is the muscle.

80
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:56,997
And when engineers want an engine to

81
00:02:56,997 --> 00:02:58,145
burn less fuel,

82
00:02:58,145 --> 00:03:00,443
one of the main things they do

83
00:03:00,443 --> 00:03:02,740
is make the fan larger and let

84
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:04,654
more air bypass the core.

85
00:03:05,005 --> 00:03:07,264
Why are the fan blades of a

86
00:03:07,264 --> 00:03:10,444
modern engine so wide and so strangely

87
00:03:10,444 --> 00:03:11,030
curved?

88
00:03:11,210 --> 00:03:13,340
Because each blade is a wing,

89
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:15,616
and its tip is spinning so fast

90
00:03:15,616 --> 00:03:17,745
that it meets the air near or

91
00:03:17,745 --> 00:03:19,581
above the speed of sound.

92
00:03:19,761 --> 00:03:23,304
The twisted, swept shape keeps the airflow

93
00:03:23,304 --> 00:03:26,003
smooth from the root to the tip,

94
00:03:26,003 --> 00:03:28,956
and controls shock waves and noise.

95
00:03:29,136 --> 00:03:31,792
Many modern fan blades are not even

96
00:03:31,792 --> 00:03:32,248
metal.

97
00:03:32,428 --> 00:03:35,137
The GE nine X uses carbon fiber

98
00:03:35,137 --> 00:03:38,896
composite blades, and only sixteen of them,

99
00:03:38,896 --> 00:03:41,606
six fewer than its predecessor,

100
00:03:41,606 --> 00:03:42,830
the GE ninety.

101
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:46,295
Fewer, wider, lighter blades mean less weight

102
00:03:46,295 --> 00:03:48,787
spinning at the front of the engine,

103
00:03:48,787 --> 00:03:51,071
and less weight in the heavy case

104
00:03:51,071 --> 00:03:52,456
that surrounds them.

105
00:03:52,636 --> 00:03:55,613
Composite blades also tolerate a lot of

106
00:03:55,613 --> 00:03:58,895
the vibration and fatigue that metal blades

107
00:03:58,895 --> 00:03:59,811
suffer from.

108
00:03:59,991 --> 00:04:02,098
According to GE Aerospace,

109
00:04:02,098 --> 00:04:04,124
the GE nine X burns up to

110
00:04:04,124 --> 00:04:06,799
ten percent less fuel than the GE

111
00:04:06,799 --> 00:04:08,501
ninety one fifteen B.

112
00:04:08,681 --> 00:04:11,189
Part of that comes from these blades.

113
00:04:11,539 --> 00:04:14,488
There is a catch with composite blades.

114
00:04:14,668 --> 00:04:17,301
They have to survive the same brutal

115
00:04:17,301 --> 00:04:18,764
tests as metal ones,

116
00:04:18,764 --> 00:04:21,324
including birds and blade failures,

117
00:04:21,324 --> 00:04:23,153
which we will get to in a

118
00:04:23,153 --> 00:04:23,665
moment.

119
00:04:23,845 --> 00:04:26,928
Engineers often protect the leading edge,

120
00:04:26,928 --> 00:04:29,409
the part that hits the air first,

121
00:04:29,409 --> 00:04:30,763
with a metal edge,

122
00:04:30,763 --> 00:04:32,793
because that is where rain,

123
00:04:32,793 --> 00:04:34,898
hail, sand and birds strike.

124
00:04:35,078 --> 00:04:37,841
The blade is a mix of materials,

125
00:04:37,841 --> 00:04:41,468
each one placed exactly where its strength

126
00:04:41,468 --> 00:04:42,764
is needed most.

127
00:04:43,114 --> 00:04:45,374
Why is the inside wall of a

128
00:04:45,374 --> 00:04:48,973
jet engine's intake covered in thousands of

129
00:04:48,973 --> 00:04:49,894
tiny holes?

130
00:04:50,074 --> 00:04:53,418
It is not decoration and not ventilation.

131
00:04:53,598 --> 00:04:56,062
It is a sound trap called an

132
00:04:56,062 --> 00:04:57,382
acoustic liner.

133
00:04:57,562 --> 00:05:00,774
Behind the perforated skin is a honeycomb

134
00:05:00,774 --> 00:05:01,949
of small cells,

135
00:05:01,949 --> 00:05:03,437
closed at the back.

136
00:05:03,617 --> 00:05:05,899
When the noise of the fan hits

137
00:05:05,899 --> 00:05:08,562
the holes, the air inside each cell

138
00:05:08,562 --> 00:05:11,453
bounces like a spring and absorbs part

139
00:05:11,453 --> 00:05:12,975
of the sound energy.

140
00:05:13,155 --> 00:05:15,615
It is the same physics that makes

141
00:05:15,615 --> 00:05:18,076
a bottle hum when you blow across

142
00:05:18,076 --> 00:05:20,761
it, used in reverse to swallow noise

143
00:05:20,761 --> 00:05:22,327
instead of making it.

144
00:05:22,677 --> 00:05:25,985
This principle is called Helmholtz resonance.

145
00:05:26,165 --> 00:05:28,687
Each cell is tuned by its size

146
00:05:28,687 --> 00:05:32,049
and depth to absorb certain frequencies,

147
00:05:32,049 --> 00:05:34,990
and some liners stack two layers of

148
00:05:34,990 --> 00:05:37,764
honeycomb to catch a wider range.

149
00:05:37,944 --> 00:05:40,694
They line both the intake and the

150
00:05:40,694 --> 00:05:41,694
bypass duct.

151
00:05:41,874 --> 00:05:44,936
The lower panels even have small slots

152
00:05:44,936 --> 00:05:46,145
to drain water,

153
00:05:46,145 --> 00:05:48,642
so ice cannot form inside them.

154
00:05:48,822 --> 00:05:51,480
And the best liners today are made

155
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:53,199
in one seamless piece,

156
00:05:53,199 --> 00:05:55,857
because every seam is an area that

157
00:05:55,857 --> 00:05:57,576
does not absorb sound.

158
00:05:57,927 --> 00:05:59,970
Why go to all this trouble?

159
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:03,085
Because the fan is the loudest part

160
00:06:03,085 --> 00:06:06,271
of a modern engine during approach and

161
00:06:06,271 --> 00:06:09,457
landing, when the airplane is low over

162
00:06:09,457 --> 00:06:09,960
homes.

163
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:12,566
The core's roar has been tamed by

164
00:06:12,566 --> 00:06:14,183
the big bypass stream,

165
00:06:14,183 --> 00:06:16,534
which wraps the hot exhaust in a

166
00:06:16,534 --> 00:06:18,151
blanket of slower air.

167
00:06:18,331 --> 00:06:20,720
What remains is the fan's buzz,

168
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:23,416
and the liners are the main defense

169
00:06:23,416 --> 00:06:24,264
against it.

170
00:06:24,444 --> 00:06:28,151
Combined with chevrons and careful blade design,

171
00:06:28,151 --> 00:06:30,777
they are why airports can sit next

172
00:06:30,777 --> 00:06:31,549
to cities.

173
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:34,448
Why is the ring around the fan

174
00:06:34,448 --> 00:06:36,741
built so incredibly strong?

175
00:06:36,921 --> 00:06:39,661
Because of one terrifying test.

176
00:06:39,841 --> 00:06:41,478
To certify an engine,

177
00:06:41,478 --> 00:06:44,673
regulators require that if a blade breaks

178
00:06:44,673 --> 00:06:47,167
off, the engine must contain it.

179
00:06:47,347 --> 00:06:50,491
So engineers prepare a blade with a

180
00:06:50,491 --> 00:06:53,186
small explosive charge inside,

181
00:06:53,186 --> 00:06:54,803
run the engine up,

182
00:06:54,803 --> 00:06:56,600
and fire the charge.

183
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:59,822
The blade flies off at full speed

184
00:06:59,822 --> 00:07:01,482
and hits the case.

185
00:07:01,662 --> 00:07:04,314
No fragment is allowed to punch through

186
00:07:04,314 --> 00:07:05,470
the outer casing.

187
00:07:05,650 --> 00:07:07,844
The engine can be destroyed.

188
00:07:08,024 --> 00:07:10,963
It just cannot throw pieces into the

189
00:07:10,963 --> 00:07:12,188
wing, the fuel,

190
00:07:12,188 --> 00:07:13,249
or the cabin.

191
00:07:13,599 --> 00:07:16,347
The rule is in the US regulations,

192
00:07:16,347 --> 00:07:19,500
part thirty-three, section ninety-four,

193
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:22,411
and Europe has the same requirement.

194
00:07:22,591 --> 00:07:24,327
There are two ways to stop a

195
00:07:24,327 --> 00:07:25,133
flying blade.

196
00:07:25,313 --> 00:07:26,382
A hard wall,

197
00:07:26,382 --> 00:07:29,589
a heavy metal ring that deflects the

198
00:07:29,589 --> 00:07:31,639
debris, or a soft wall,

199
00:07:31,639 --> 00:07:34,578
a thick wrap of strong fiber like

200
00:07:34,578 --> 00:07:38,053
aramid, which catches the pieces like a

201
00:07:38,053 --> 00:07:38,409
net.

202
00:07:38,589 --> 00:07:41,726
Composite and hollow blades help here too,

203
00:07:41,726 --> 00:07:44,416
because a lighter blade is easier to

204
00:07:44,416 --> 00:07:44,789
stop.

205
00:07:45,139 --> 00:07:48,042
This matters because a blade that escapes

206
00:07:48,042 --> 00:07:49,458
can be catastrophic.

207
00:07:49,638 --> 00:07:53,227
Engine fragments have punctured fuel tanks,

208
00:07:53,227 --> 00:07:54,897
cut hydraulic lines,

209
00:07:54,897 --> 00:07:57,985
and broken windows in real accidents.

210
00:07:58,165 --> 00:08:01,656
Containment is what turns a frightening engine

211
00:08:01,656 --> 00:08:04,691
failure into a story the passengers tell

212
00:08:04,691 --> 00:08:07,119
at dinner, about a loud bang and

213
00:08:07,119 --> 00:08:09,775
a diversion, instead of a disaster.

214
00:08:09,955 --> 00:08:12,995
That is why the explosive test exists,

215
00:08:12,995 --> 00:08:15,555
and why every engine has to pass

216
00:08:15,555 --> 00:08:15,795
it.

217
00:08:16,145 --> 00:08:19,012
Why do engineers fire birds into brand

218
00:08:19,012 --> 00:08:21,276
new jet engines with a cannon?

219
00:08:21,456 --> 00:08:23,906
Because real birds will hit them.

220
00:08:24,086 --> 00:08:26,141
The US rules spell it out.

221
00:08:26,321 --> 00:08:29,344
A large engine must survive a single

222
00:08:29,344 --> 00:08:32,535
bird of about eight pounds hitting its

223
00:08:32,535 --> 00:08:34,634
fan at two hundred knots,

224
00:08:34,634 --> 00:08:38,161
and shut down safely without catching fire

225
00:08:38,161 --> 00:08:39,672
or breaking loose.

226
00:08:39,852 --> 00:08:41,791
Then there is the flock test.

227
00:08:41,971 --> 00:08:44,555
Several medium birds are fired in,

228
00:08:44,555 --> 00:08:47,292
and the engine must keep running for

229
00:08:47,292 --> 00:08:49,648
about twenty minutes afterward,

230
00:08:49,648 --> 00:08:51,700
at changing power settings,

231
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:53,980
like it would on a real flight

232
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:55,880
returning to the airport.

233
00:08:56,230 --> 00:08:59,039
The requirement depends on how big the

234
00:08:59,039 --> 00:09:00,444
engine's intake is.

235
00:09:00,624 --> 00:09:02,342
The bigger the opening,

236
00:09:02,342 --> 00:09:05,256
the bigger and more numerous the birds.

237
00:09:05,436 --> 00:09:07,366
For the largest engines,

238
00:09:07,366 --> 00:09:10,020
the large single bird test uses a

239
00:09:10,020 --> 00:09:13,076
bird of eight point zero three pounds.

240
00:09:13,256 --> 00:09:14,918
During the flock test,

241
00:09:14,918 --> 00:09:18,244
the engine cannot lose more than twenty-five

242
00:09:18,244 --> 00:09:21,040
percent of its thrust and cannot shut

243
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:21,418
down.

244
00:09:21,598 --> 00:09:25,099
Engine makers use a compressed air launcher,

245
00:09:25,099 --> 00:09:27,248
often called a bird cannon,

246
00:09:27,248 --> 00:09:29,635
to fire them at the exact spot

247
00:09:29,635 --> 00:09:32,102
on the blades that would do the

248
00:09:32,102 --> 00:09:33,057
most damage.

249
00:09:33,407 --> 00:09:35,206
Why so strict?

250
00:09:35,386 --> 00:09:38,173
Because sometimes the worst happens.

251
00:09:38,353 --> 00:09:40,078
In two thousand nine,

252
00:09:40,078 --> 00:09:43,530
US Airways flight fifteen forty-nine hit a

253
00:09:43,530 --> 00:09:46,817
flock of Canada geese after takeoff from

254
00:09:46,817 --> 00:09:49,939
New York, and both engines lost power.

255
00:09:50,119 --> 00:09:52,437
Geese can weigh up to around ten

256
00:09:52,437 --> 00:09:56,203
pounds, heavier than the biggest certification bird.

257
00:09:56,383 --> 00:09:57,923
After that flight,

258
00:09:57,923 --> 00:10:01,774
testing for multiple bird ingestion became an

259
00:10:01,774 --> 00:10:03,314
even bigger focus.

260
00:10:03,494 --> 00:10:06,385
Nature does not read the regulations.

261
00:10:06,735 --> 00:10:09,914
Why are the turbine blades deep inside

262
00:10:09,914 --> 00:10:12,841
a jet engine covered in tiny holes?

263
00:10:13,021 --> 00:10:15,416
Because the gas hitting them is hotter

264
00:10:15,416 --> 00:10:17,747
than the melting point of the blades.

265
00:10:17,927 --> 00:10:20,916
The gas entering the turbine can reach

266
00:10:20,916 --> 00:10:23,906
about sixteen hundred degrees Celsius.

267
00:10:24,086 --> 00:10:26,446
The nickel alloy of the blades melts

268
00:10:26,446 --> 00:10:29,461
at roughly twelve hundred to fourteen hundred.

269
00:10:29,641 --> 00:10:31,429
So the blades are hollow.

270
00:10:31,609 --> 00:10:35,024
Cooler air from the compressor flows through

271
00:10:35,024 --> 00:10:37,895
channels inside them and comes out of

272
00:10:37,895 --> 00:10:39,215
those tiny holes,

273
00:10:39,215 --> 00:10:42,241
wrapping the blade in a thin protective

274
00:10:42,241 --> 00:10:43,173
film of air.

275
00:10:43,353 --> 00:10:46,402
The metal survives because it never actually

276
00:10:46,402 --> 00:10:47,927
touches the full heat.

277
00:10:48,277 --> 00:10:51,179
This trick is called film cooling.

278
00:10:51,359 --> 00:10:54,260
The cooling air itself is around seven

279
00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:57,391
hundred to eight hundred degrees Celsius,

280
00:10:57,391 --> 00:10:58,689
which sounds hot,

281
00:10:58,689 --> 00:11:01,514
but compared with the gas outside the

282
00:11:01,514 --> 00:11:02,888
blade, it is cold.

283
00:11:03,068 --> 00:11:06,519
The internal channels are formed during casting,

284
00:11:06,519 --> 00:11:09,108
using ceramic cores inside the mold,

285
00:11:09,108 --> 00:11:11,984
and the holes are drilled afterward with

286
00:11:11,984 --> 00:11:13,494
incredible precision.

287
00:11:13,674 --> 00:11:15,986
Each blade is a tiny piece of

288
00:11:15,986 --> 00:11:18,936
plumbing, built to survive in a place

289
00:11:18,936 --> 00:11:22,126
where its own material should be liquid.

290
00:11:22,476 --> 00:11:25,387
Some of the most advanced turbine blades

291
00:11:25,387 --> 00:11:27,062
take this even further.

292
00:11:27,242 --> 00:11:29,530
They are cast as a single crystal

293
00:11:29,530 --> 00:11:31,957
of metal, with no grain boundaries,

294
00:11:31,957 --> 00:11:34,870
the tiny weak lines between crystals where

295
00:11:34,870 --> 00:11:37,714
cracks like to start at high temperature.

296
00:11:37,894 --> 00:11:40,794
Rolls-Royce has cast blades this way in

297
00:11:40,794 --> 00:11:44,066
Derby, England, since the nineteen eighties,

298
00:11:44,066 --> 00:11:47,263
about three hundred thousand blades a year.

299
00:11:47,443 --> 00:11:50,083
The metal is poured at around fifteen

300
00:11:50,083 --> 00:11:51,795
hundred degrees Celsius,

301
00:11:51,795 --> 00:11:54,220
and the mold is slowly pulled into

302
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:56,717
a cold chamber so the metal freezes

303
00:11:56,717 --> 00:11:57,787
as one crystal.

304
00:11:58,138 --> 00:12:00,505
Why are the hottest parts of a

305
00:12:00,505 --> 00:12:02,793
jet engine coated in ceramic?

306
00:12:02,973 --> 00:12:06,164
Because ceramic is a terrible conductor of

307
00:12:06,164 --> 00:12:08,747
heat, and that is exactly what you

308
00:12:08,747 --> 00:12:09,126
want.

309
00:12:09,306 --> 00:12:12,354
A thermal barrier coating about a quarter

310
00:12:12,354 --> 00:12:15,104
of a millimeter thick is sprayed onto

311
00:12:15,104 --> 00:12:16,516
the turbine blades.

312
00:12:16,696 --> 00:12:19,611
That thin layer keeps the metal underneath

313
00:12:19,611 --> 00:12:22,457
roughly a hundred degrees cooler than its

314
00:12:22,457 --> 00:12:23,012
surface.

315
00:12:23,192 --> 00:12:25,600
A hundred degrees may not sound like

316
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:27,741
much, but at these temperatures,

317
00:12:27,741 --> 00:12:30,015
it can be the difference between a

318
00:12:30,015 --> 00:12:32,290
blade that lasts for years and one

319
00:12:32,290 --> 00:12:33,026
that fails.

320
00:12:33,376 --> 00:12:35,489
The newest engines go further.

321
00:12:35,669 --> 00:12:37,756
According to GE Aerospace,

322
00:12:37,756 --> 00:12:39,924
the GE nine X has more than

323
00:12:39,924 --> 00:12:43,296
one hundred parts made from ceramic matrix

324
00:12:43,296 --> 00:12:47,070
composites, materials that can handle more heat

325
00:12:47,070 --> 00:12:49,960
than metal while weighing much less.

326
00:12:50,140 --> 00:12:53,277
Hotter running engines are more efficient,

327
00:12:53,277 --> 00:12:56,413
so every material that tolerates more heat

328
00:12:56,413 --> 00:12:59,624
lets engineers squeeze more power from less

329
00:12:59,624 --> 00:12:59,997
fuel.

330
00:13:00,177 --> 00:13:02,435
The history of the jet engine is,

331
00:13:02,435 --> 00:13:03,392
in large part,

332
00:13:03,392 --> 00:13:05,239
a history of fighting heat.

333
00:13:05,590 --> 00:13:07,655
Why does the back of a jet

334
00:13:07,655 --> 00:13:10,675
engine slide open right after landing?

335
00:13:10,855 --> 00:13:12,887
That is the thrust reverser.

336
00:13:13,067 --> 00:13:15,006
On most modern engines,

337
00:13:15,006 --> 00:13:16,777
a sleeve slides back,

338
00:13:16,777 --> 00:13:19,813
blocker doors close the normal exit,

339
00:13:19,813 --> 00:13:22,259
and the fan air is forced out

340
00:13:22,259 --> 00:13:25,379
through angled vanes called cascades,

341
00:13:25,379 --> 00:13:26,813
pointing forward.

342
00:13:26,993 --> 00:13:30,062
The engine is still blowing backward through

343
00:13:30,062 --> 00:13:32,154
its core, but most of its push

344
00:13:32,154 --> 00:13:33,759
now goes the other way,

345
00:13:33,759 --> 00:13:35,781
helping to slow the airplane.

346
00:13:35,961 --> 00:13:38,099
According to Pilot Institute,

347
00:13:38,099 --> 00:13:41,564
reverse thrust can shorten the landing distance

348
00:13:41,564 --> 00:13:43,334
by up to twenty percent.

349
00:13:43,514 --> 00:13:45,174
And it saves the brakes.

350
00:13:45,524 --> 00:13:48,754
But many landing distance calculations do not

351
00:13:48,754 --> 00:13:50,620
count on reversers at all,

352
00:13:50,620 --> 00:13:52,845
because an engine might be out,

353
00:13:52,845 --> 00:13:55,357
so the brakes alone must be enough.

354
00:13:55,537 --> 00:13:57,977
And reversers have to stay locked in

355
00:13:57,977 --> 00:13:58,451
flight.

356
00:13:58,631 --> 00:14:00,492
In nineteen ninety-one,

357
00:14:00,492 --> 00:14:03,324
on Lauda Air flight zero zero four,

358
00:14:03,324 --> 00:14:07,288
a Boeing seven sixty-seven's left thrust reverser

359
00:14:07,288 --> 00:14:10,443
deployed in flight while the engine was

360
00:14:10,443 --> 00:14:13,275
at high power during the climb from

361
00:14:13,275 --> 00:14:13,922
Bangkok.

362
00:14:14,102 --> 00:14:16,618
The airplane went out of control,

363
00:14:16,618 --> 00:14:18,676
and everyone on board died.

364
00:14:18,856 --> 00:14:21,485
Boeing then added a mechanical lock to

365
00:14:21,485 --> 00:14:22,246
the system.

366
00:14:22,596 --> 00:14:24,670
Where does the air you breathe on

367
00:14:24,670 --> 00:14:26,241
most airliners come from?

368
00:14:26,421 --> 00:14:27,605
From the engines.

369
00:14:27,785 --> 00:14:29,810
Since the nineteen fifties,

370
00:14:29,810 --> 00:14:32,734
jets have taken compressed air from the

371
00:14:32,734 --> 00:14:35,358
compressor stages of their engines,

372
00:14:35,358 --> 00:14:36,632
called bleed air.

373
00:14:36,812 --> 00:14:39,353
It is already squeezed and hot,

374
00:14:39,353 --> 00:14:42,630
so after cooling in the air conditioning

375
00:14:42,630 --> 00:14:45,253
packs, it pressurizes the cabin.

376
00:14:45,433 --> 00:14:48,184
The same hot air also heats the

377
00:14:48,184 --> 00:14:51,201
wings to stop ice and powers other

378
00:14:51,201 --> 00:14:51,911
systems.

379
00:14:52,091 --> 00:14:54,309
It is clean air from outside.

380
00:14:54,489 --> 00:14:56,607
It is pulled in at the front,

381
00:14:56,607 --> 00:14:58,506
before any fuel is burned.

382
00:14:58,856 --> 00:15:00,736
But bleed air is not free.

383
00:15:00,916 --> 00:15:03,043
Every bit of air taken out of

384
00:15:03,043 --> 00:15:05,463
the compressor is air that is not

385
00:15:05,463 --> 00:15:06,489
making thrust.

386
00:15:06,669 --> 00:15:09,084
So the Boeing seven eighty-seven took a

387
00:15:09,084 --> 00:15:10,013
different path.

388
00:15:10,193 --> 00:15:13,752
It uses electric compressors to pressurize the

389
00:15:13,752 --> 00:15:17,001
cabin, and electric heating mats to de-ice

390
00:15:17,001 --> 00:15:17,774
its wings.

391
00:15:17,954 --> 00:15:21,499
Boeing claimed about a three percent improvement

392
00:15:21,499 --> 00:15:23,788
in fuel burn from this no bleed

393
00:15:23,788 --> 00:15:24,305
design.

394
00:15:24,485 --> 00:15:26,294
To make that possible,

395
00:15:26,294 --> 00:15:29,419
each engine drives two big generators.

396
00:15:29,769 --> 00:15:32,496
There is a quiet engineering trade-off hidden

397
00:15:32,496 --> 00:15:32,799
here.

398
00:15:32,979 --> 00:15:35,919
The engine's compressor is designed to make

399
00:15:35,919 --> 00:15:38,723
thrust, and every system that borrows its

400
00:15:38,723 --> 00:15:41,048
air makes it work a little harder.

401
00:15:41,228 --> 00:15:45,265
Moving to electric systems means bigger generators

402
00:15:45,265 --> 00:15:46,556
and more wiring,

403
00:15:46,556 --> 00:15:48,898
but it lets the engine do one

404
00:15:48,898 --> 00:15:50,028
job very well.

405
00:15:50,208 --> 00:15:53,578
Newer airliners are moving in this direction,

406
00:15:53,578 --> 00:15:54,552
step by step,

407
00:15:54,552 --> 00:15:57,847
toward what engineers call the more electric

408
00:15:57,847 --> 00:15:58,521
aircraft.

409
00:15:58,871 --> 00:16:01,144
Why is a jet engine attached to

410
00:16:01,144 --> 00:16:03,784
the wing with pins that are designed

411
00:16:03,784 --> 00:16:04,443
to break?

412
00:16:04,623 --> 00:16:06,439
They are called fuse pins,

413
00:16:06,439 --> 00:16:08,393
and like an electrical fuse,

414
00:16:08,393 --> 00:16:09,860
they fail on purpose.

415
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:13,291
If an engine suffers a huge overload,

416
00:16:13,291 --> 00:16:16,191
for example in a crash landing or

417
00:16:16,191 --> 00:16:17,773
a violent seizure,

418
00:16:17,773 --> 00:16:19,003
the pins snap,

419
00:16:19,003 --> 00:16:22,079
and the engine breaks away cleanly.

420
00:16:22,259 --> 00:16:24,775
The idea is to protect the wing,

421
00:16:24,775 --> 00:16:27,841
and especially the fuel tank inside it,

422
00:16:27,841 --> 00:16:29,492
from being torn open.

423
00:16:29,672 --> 00:16:31,715
Losing an engine is bad.

424
00:16:31,895 --> 00:16:34,475
Losing the wing and its fuel is

425
00:16:34,475 --> 00:16:35,390
much worse.

426
00:16:35,740 --> 00:16:37,364
But a fuse must break only when

427
00:16:37,364 --> 00:16:37,887
it should.

428
00:16:38,067 --> 00:16:40,140
On the fourth of October,

429
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:43,623
nineteen ninety-two, an El Al Boeing seven

430
00:16:43,623 --> 00:16:47,437
forty-seven freighter took off from Amsterdam.

431
00:16:47,617 --> 00:16:50,157
Fuse pins on the number three engine

432
00:16:50,157 --> 00:16:52,203
had developed fatigue cracks.

433
00:16:52,383 --> 00:16:54,082
The engine broke off,

434
00:16:54,082 --> 00:16:56,024
struck the outer engine,

435
00:16:56,024 --> 00:16:57,723
and tore it away too.

436
00:16:57,903 --> 00:17:01,545
The airplane crashed into apartment buildings in

437
00:17:01,545 --> 00:17:03,441
the Bijlmermeer district.

438
00:17:03,621 --> 00:17:06,022
Four people on board and at least

439
00:17:06,022 --> 00:17:08,277
forty-three on the ground died.

440
00:17:08,627 --> 00:17:12,125
The Dutch investigation found that the pylon's

441
00:17:12,125 --> 00:17:15,243
design and certification were inadequate,

442
00:17:15,243 --> 00:17:18,285
and that inspections had failed to catch

443
00:17:18,285 --> 00:17:19,122
the cracks.

444
00:17:19,302 --> 00:17:22,780
Boeing ordered the engines and pylons removed

445
00:17:22,780 --> 00:17:25,948
from seven forty-sevens so the pins could

446
00:17:25,948 --> 00:17:29,426
be inspected, and cracked pins were replaced.

447
00:17:29,606 --> 00:17:32,420
The idea of a part that breaks

448
00:17:32,420 --> 00:17:34,296
on purpose survived.

449
00:17:34,476 --> 00:17:36,567
But the lesson was that it must

450
00:17:36,567 --> 00:17:39,333
be strong enough for every normal flight,

451
00:17:39,333 --> 00:17:40,142
for decades.

452
00:17:40,492 --> 00:17:42,743
Why does a jet engine also make

453
00:17:42,743 --> 00:17:43,615
electricity?

454
00:17:43,795 --> 00:17:47,009
Because the airplane needs a power plant

455
00:17:47,009 --> 00:17:47,893
in the sky.

456
00:17:48,073 --> 00:17:50,960
Underneath the engine is a gearbox,

457
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:53,352
driven by the engine's shaft,

458
00:17:53,352 --> 00:17:55,991
that spins pumps and generators.

459
00:17:56,171 --> 00:17:57,901
They power the lights,

460
00:17:57,901 --> 00:17:59,867
the screens, the galleys,

461
00:17:59,867 --> 00:18:01,518
the flight computers,

462
00:18:01,518 --> 00:18:03,012
and on modern jets,

463
00:18:03,012 --> 00:18:03,799
much more.

464
00:18:03,979 --> 00:18:06,383
On the Boeing seven eighty-seven,

465
00:18:06,383 --> 00:18:09,297
each engine drives two generators of two

466
00:18:09,297 --> 00:18:11,920
hundred fifty kilovolt amperes each.

467
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:15,205
That is one thousand kilovolt amperes from

468
00:18:15,205 --> 00:18:16,387
the two engines,

469
00:18:16,387 --> 00:18:18,753
far more than older jets needed.

470
00:18:19,103 --> 00:18:20,292
On the ground,

471
00:18:20,292 --> 00:18:22,417
before the engines start,

472
00:18:22,417 --> 00:18:25,476
the auxiliary power unit in the tail

473
00:18:25,476 --> 00:18:27,175
takes over that job,

474
00:18:27,175 --> 00:18:29,215
with its own generators.

475
00:18:29,395 --> 00:18:30,288
In the air,

476
00:18:30,288 --> 00:18:32,074
if every engine stops,

477
00:18:32,074 --> 00:18:34,428
the ram air turbine we saw in

478
00:18:34,428 --> 00:18:37,350
our last airplane video drops out to

479
00:18:37,350 --> 00:18:39,461
keep the essentials alive.

480
00:18:39,641 --> 00:18:43,135
Electricity on an airliner has several layers

481
00:18:43,135 --> 00:18:46,319
of backup, because almost everything on a

482
00:18:46,319 --> 00:18:49,347
modern jet, from the flight controls to

483
00:18:49,347 --> 00:18:50,512
the fuel pumps,

484
00:18:50,512 --> 00:18:51,599
depends on it.

485
00:18:51,949 --> 00:18:54,004
So here are the eleven.

486
00:18:54,184 --> 00:18:56,294
A fan that makes most of the

487
00:18:56,294 --> 00:18:59,083
thrust, with curved composite blades.

488
00:18:59,263 --> 00:19:02,270
Holes in the intake that swallow sound.

489
00:19:02,450 --> 00:19:05,408
A ring that catches its own exploding

490
00:19:05,408 --> 00:19:08,126
blade, and a test that fires birds

491
00:19:08,126 --> 00:19:08,766
into it.

492
00:19:08,946 --> 00:19:12,092
Blades with holes that survive above their

493
00:19:12,092 --> 00:19:15,087
melting point, and ceramic armor on top.

494
00:19:15,267 --> 00:19:18,193
Doors that push backward on landing,

495
00:19:18,193 --> 00:19:19,737
air for your lungs,

496
00:19:19,737 --> 00:19:21,607
pins designed to break,

497
00:19:21,607 --> 00:19:24,532
and generators that make it a flying

498
00:19:24,532 --> 00:19:25,507
power plant.

499
00:19:25,858 --> 00:19:28,180
A jet engine looks like a simple

500
00:19:28,180 --> 00:19:29,341
tube with a fan.

501
00:19:29,521 --> 00:19:32,182
It is really dozens of solutions to

502
00:19:32,182 --> 00:19:33,627
dozens of problems,

503
00:19:33,627 --> 00:19:35,604
stacked inside each other.

504
00:19:35,784 --> 00:19:38,928
If you want more machines explained part

505
00:19:38,928 --> 00:19:41,914
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

506
00:19:42,094 --> 00:19:44,032
And tell us in the comments which

507
00:19:44,032 --> 00:19:45,969
machine you want us to take apart

508
00:19:45,969 --> 00:19:46,262
next.

509
00:19:46,442 --> 00:19:48,300
Rockets are coming soon.
